,"name":"RYM Top — Hip Hop — Gangstarap","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":100,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":null},{"id":215424088090262,"name":"AOTY Must Hear 1980s","src":"charts","grp":"AOTY","list_year":null,"rank":103,"year":1984,"note":null,"total":196,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The live soundtrack album for the concert film of the same name, released in 1984. It initially saw modest chart success, peaking at number 41, lower than the band's five previous LPs. However, it became a \"long-distance runner,\" staying on the Billboard album chart for nearly two years—longer than any other Talking Heads album—and eventually went double platinum, serving as a de facto greatest hits compilation before the band released any official one. \"But Stop Making Sense was the long-distance runner of their albums, serving as a de facto Talking Heads greatest hits, years before the group put out any such compilation.\"","year":1984,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"This Ain’t No Party! Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f6b39d31c85883dc9de"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Live Soundtrack LP","body":"The live soundtrack album for the concert film of the same name, released in 1984. It initially saw modest chart success, peaking at number 41, lower than the band's five previous LPs. However, it became a \"long-distance runner,\" staying on the Billboard album chart for nearly two years—longer than any other Talking Heads album—and eventually went double platinum, serving as a de facto greatest hits compilation before the band released any official one. \"But Stop Making Sense was the long-distance runner of their albums, serving as a de facto Talking Heads greatest hits, years before the group put out any such compilation.\"","year":1984,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Triv